{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199 a period marked by significant political and military challenges. He was a prominent figure in the Crusader states navigating complex relationships with both Christian and Muslim powers. His reign witnessed both successes and setbacks solidifying his legacy as a capable albeit controversial ruler.\n\n*Early Life and Accession* Raymond III was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were dominated by consolidating his authority within the County facing internal opposition and external threats. The region was a strategic crossroads between Christian and Muslim territories constantly subject to raids and sieges. Raymond III quickly established himself as a skilled military leader necessary for survival in this volatile environment. He faced challenges from rival claimants to the throne and had to skillfully manage the local population ensuring loyalty and stability.\n\nRaymond III's reign was characterized by shrewd diplomacy and military campaigns. He engaged in frequent conflicts with the Zengid dynasty rulers of Syria particularly Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. He also maintained relations with the Kingdom of Jerusalem seeking alliances and support against common enemies. His military campaigns often aimed to expand the County's territory or secure vital trade routes. He understood the importance of maintaining a strong defensive posture building fortifications and training a well-equipped army. He also fostered economic growth within the County promoting trade and agriculture.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* A key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his adeptness at diplomacy. He forged alliances with various powers including the Kingdom of Jerusalem the Principality of Antioch and even certain Muslim rulers when it served his interests. His relationships with the Crusaders were often strained due to disagreements over strategy and resources. He frequently sought to balance the interests of the Crusader states with those of the local Muslim rulers a delicate balancing act that required considerable skill. He understood that long-term survival depended on maintaining a degree of cooperation with his neighbors even those considered enemies.\n\n*Later Years and Legacy* In his later years Raymond III faced increasing pressure from Saladin who posed a significant threat to the Crusader states. He attempted to negotiate with Saladin seeking to maintain a degree of autonomy for the County of Tripoli. However Saladin's growing power ultimately proved too great. Raymond III died in 1199 leaving the County to his son Raymond IV. His reign is viewed as a period of stability and prosperity for Tripoli despite the constant threats it faced. He is remembered as a capable ruler a skilled diplomat and a courageous military leader who defended his County against overwhelming odds. His legacy highlights the challenges and complexities of ruling a Crusader state in the 12th and 13th centuries.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0011152960360050201}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.26702672243118286}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.18335619568824768}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.04817276448011398}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00023457636416424066}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.5748875486897305e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.821448318485636e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.8132995794294402e-05}, {"token": 